        "title": "The Witness's Frame",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "This volume covers three kinds of people who changed what was possible. Political reformers rewrote rules. Social reformers made invisible harm visible. Visionaries redefined what the system was *for*. They look different on the surface — Solon drafting debt-relief statutes, Nightingale drawing polar area diagrams, Turing proving the halting problem — but they share a structural property: each one found the leverage point that mattered in their system and pushed."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-WITNESS-FRAME.md#005",
      "path": [
        "00-WITNESS-FRAME.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:political-reformers:00-witness-frame, git-history:political-reformers:00-witness-frame]",
        "canonical_path": "political-reformers/00-WITNESS-FRAME.md",
        "concepts": "[witness, frame]",
        "current_path": "political-reformers/00-WITNESS-FRAME.md",
        "id": "maxim:political-reformers:witness-frame",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "political-reformers",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[witness, frame]",
        "section": "political-reformers",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "The Witness's Frame",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Donella Meadows formalized this intuition. In \"Leverage Points: Places to Intervene in a System\" (1999), she ranked twelve places where you can push on a complex system, from weakest to strongest. The deeper you go, the harder the intervention — and the more it transforms."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-WITNESS-FRAME.md#006",
      "path": [
        "00-WITNESS-FRAME.md"
      ],
